{"name":"Banner Gold","hex":"#a28557","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"banner","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A flag or standard used by a military commander, monarch or nation.","The military unit under such a flag or standard."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Exceptional; very good."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/banner"},{"term":"gold","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A heavy yellow elemental metal of great value, with atomic number 79.","A coin or coinage made of this material, or supposedly so."]},{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["☉ (alchemy)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gold"}],"entries":[{"title":"Banner","blocks":[{"text":"A banner is a flag or another piece of cloth bearing a symbol, logo, slogan or another message. A flag whose design is the same as the shield in a coat of arms is a banner of arms. A bar-shaped piece of advertising material sporting a name, slogan, or other marketing message is also a banner. Banners may hang from horizontal or vertical flagpoles, be stretched between two poles, be attached to windows or buildings, or be towed behind aircraft."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Banners_of_Knights_of_the_Thistle.jpg/330px-Banners_of_Knights_of_the_Thistle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Banner"},{"title":"Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/330px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Wolsey_banner.jpg/120px-Wolsey_banner.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/57/Wolsey_banner.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Wolsey_banner.jpg/120px-Wolsey_banner.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWolsey_banner.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Sanctus_Gummarus_Lyrmensis_04.JPG/500px-Sanctus_Gummarus_Lyrmensis_04.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Sanctus_Gummarus_Lyrmensis_04.JPG/500px-Sanctus_Gummarus_Lyrmensis_04.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Religious banners of Catholic brotherhoods in Lier, Belgium","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASanctus_Gummarus_Lyrmensis_04.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Kirkkoliput.jpg/500px-Kirkkoliput.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Kirkkoliput.jpg/500px-Kirkkoliput.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm